by the time the Second World War ended  in 1945 Europe was in ruins the daunting  task of rebuilding and planning a better  future began the 1950s brought a spirit  of new beginnings and robert schumann  the french foreign minister gave a  speech outlining a vision of France West  Germany Italy and the Benelux countries  sharing strategic resources in order to  build a lasting peace in Europe when you  later  at the Treaty of Paris the European Coal  and Steel community was founded a common  steel and coal market was freely set  market prices and without import or  export duties the first manifestation of  the European community was born the need  for an external representation for the  newly formed European community was  apparent right from the start when John  mone set up a transatlantic office in  1954 the first foundation stone of the  external relations of the European  community was laid Europe's first steps  to be present in the wider world  coincided with the dismantling of  European empires beginning in 1957 with  the independence of Ghana the Treaty of  Rome that year recognized the  relationship of the founding six members  to their overseas territories mainly in  Africa a five-year development fund was  set up this fund was used for capital  aid programs to develop infrastructure  like roads hospitals and schools  contract teams administering the funds  were typically drawn from European  engineering consultancies resident in  the countries but the old colonial  empires were changing both for the  European colonial powers and the newly  independent nations the Commission's  contract teams soon found themselves  dealing with new issues especially in  Africa where nearly independent  countries sought to establish diplomatic  relations with Europe during the 1960's  a new relationship was formalized under  the unday conventions  and a post-colonial development  agreement was established the European  Development Fund the contract teams were  replaced by a new agency the European  agency for cooperation from 1965 onwards  21 offices were established in the  Associated countries these officers were  charged with implementing the grant aid  of the new fund dealing mainly with  internal development issues in Europe  the effects of the cold war intensified  with the building of what was to become  the iconic symbol of the Cold War the  Berlin Wall while in Western Europe  walls were coming down in 1968 the  customs union was completed and goods  now passed freely and unchecked across  the borders of the Member States in the  mid-1970s the first law made convention  was signed between Europe and forty six  founding African Caribbean and Pacific  states  lo mein was a modern International  partnership convention  replacing the old post-colonial model  long way redefined the relationship  between Europe and developing countries  it addressed more than just development  cooperation now trade regional  integration political issues and  cultural cooperation were on the agenda  European delegation offices were charged  specifically with the responsibility of  implementing the Roma Convention and to  the number of these delegations doubled  the European community continued to grow  with the accession of Ireland the UK and  Denmark the mid-70s also saw the signing  of community protocols with eight  southern and eastern Mediterranean  countries the relationship and attention  given to Latin America was strengthened  when Spain and Portugal joined stronger  ties were also developed in Asia Europe  became more professional in its dealings  with the rest of the world an  international European personality was  steadily being created to deal with the  changing world by 1980 there were 50  delegation offices around the world  delegations opened at the rate of five a  year as Europe took an increasing role  in world affairs the need for a common  foreign policy became more pressing and  a common foreign and security policy was  eventually formalized in 1992 by the  Maastricht Treaty the end of the Cold  War forced further changes to foreign  policy in light of the problems faced by  Europe in reacting to the conflicts that  erupted after the fall of the Berlin  Wall in 1989 particularly in the former  Yugoslavia the EU created a European  security and defence policy a rethink of  the external service led to a major  overhaul in 1998 when the Amsterdam  treaty established the position of High  Representative we have to move towards a  more active and less declaratory foreign  policy I would head to be matched by  deeds Europe began to intensify its  efforts to play an international  political and security role more in line  with its economic status military or  police forces were now sent to areas of  crisis to carry out humanitarian  operations peacekeeping crisis  management and even peacemaking military  action could also be carried out by an  EU Rapid Reaction Force the fall of the  communist regimes also demanded a  response  the EU set up programs to help the  transition to democratic market  orientated economies the implementation  of these programs coincided with the  establishment of new delegation offices  across Central and Eastern Europe Europe  also looked to its southern and Middle  Eastern neighbours in 1995 the 15 member  states of the EU came together with 12  governments from the region the  euro-mediterranean partnership promoted  democracy human rights and cooperation  among the partners now in 2007 the EU  has expanded to 27 member states  effectively pushing the European border  further east to a new set of neighbors  the European neighbourhood policy  emerged to help the --use neighbors  benefit from the possibilities offered  by an enlarged EU and to support their  own reforms today the EU is the world's  largest donor of development assistance  accounting for more than half of the  world's official aid the external  service has 130 delegations and 5000  members of staff presenting explaining  and implementing the --use external  policy today Europe has a global reach  facilitating trade and development  supporting human rights and free  elections in order to promote democratic  values we have to work with all our  partners in order to make issues go  forward and thus bring the international  community together and the more we can  do the more effective we will be this is  what we call effective multilateralism  for 50 years the European dream has  ensured peace on the continent in a  world of global threats and challenges  the Europe of the 21st century will  continue to tackle the current concerns  of its citizens such as the economy  quality of life and security it will  also become a stronger global player  committed to multilateralism  using its foreign policy tools of aid  trade and economic agreements to promote  human rights democracy freedom and  justice throughout the world
 

